  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCurrent surface analysis shows the second and stronger cold front \nmoving through the region with a high pressure system building ahead \nto our north. This will allow for a very pleasant\nafternoon/evening with highs in the 60s (terrain) to mid 70s \n(valleys)along with a light northerly breeze. Drier air will \nbegin to filter in lowering dewpoints into the 50s through this \nevening, however some patchy fog will still be possible in the \ntypical river valleys and near wetlands. With the ADKs being \nclosest to the center of the surface high, favorable radiational\ncooling conditions will lower tonight's low temperatures for \nhigher terrain areas into the mid 30s which may allow for some \npatchy frost to develop in the sheltered areas, but coverage is \nnot expected to be widespread enough to warrant frost advisory \nissuance. With the surface high still sliding by to our north \ntomorrow, it is expected to be a very nice early-fall day with \nhighs generally in the 60s topping out near 70 in the Mid-Hudson\nValley. Dewpoints in the 30s and 40s will make for very \ncomfortable conditions throughout the region. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nMoisture begins to return late Saturday night into Sunday, as \nan upper level shortwave develops over the Great Lakes and  \ntrack through the region timed with a low pressure system and \nassociated frontal boundary stalling to our south along the \ncoast. These will be the primary drivers for synoptic-scale \nforcing and isentropic lift, however a strengthening jet streak \nto our north, putting most of the region in the right rear \nentrance region, will additionally aid with divergence aloft. \nThe frontal boundary's warm sector will remain to our south \nhowever strong mid and low level warm air advection will \ntransport sufficient moisture northwards into our region. The \nlatest model guidance/CAMs have PWATs Sunday afternoon ranging \nfrom 1.5 to 2.0\" with the NAEFS ensemble showing PWAT anomalies \nincreasing to +1 to + 1.5\" STDEV by Sunday afternoon, which is \njust slightly above the daily climatological mean value for our \nregion. Widespread rain and showers are expected to begin \nSaturday night and become steady through much of Sunday before \nexiting Sunday night. There is still some uncertainty of where \nthe heaviest rainfall axis will be, but the majority of models \nhave the heaviest rain falling across southern areas where the \nbest mid-level forcing will be. Total rainfall amounts generally\nlook to be 0.25 to 0.75\" north of I-90, with 1 to 1.5\" south, \nexcept near I-84 where some locally higher amounts up to around \n2\" will be possible. \n\nProbabilistic guidance has a 60 to 70% chance for >1\" and a 30 to 40% \nchance for >2\" areas south of I-90. There is a 20 to 30% chance for \n>1\" areas north of I-90. WPC has placed a Marginal ERO for mainly I-\n90 south with a Slight ERO clipping the southern portions of Ulster, \nDutchess, and Litchfield counties. Given that 1-hr FFG values are \ngenerally >2\", rain will be stratiform in nature, and little to no \ninstability, there is a very low chance for hydro threats. However \nsome nuisance ponding of water in the typical low-lying and poor \ndrainage areas is possible. Due to widespread rain/wetbulbing, highs \nSunday may not make it out of the 50s for many areas. \n\nFrom previous discussion:\n\nShowers should come to an end Sunday night as a strengthening sfc \nhigh builds into southeastern Canada beneath confluent upper flow. \nThis will help push the frontal boundary back south, so Monday looks \nrelatively dry at this time. For Tuesday through the middle of next \nweek, there is some uncertainty if the sfc high will continue to \nsuppress this boundary to our south or if the sfc high will retreat \nenough that the boundary is able to lift north and bring some \nrenewed shower chances to our southern areas. Given the strength of \nthe sfc high, we are leaning towards the drier solution. With our \narea expected to be on the cool side of the frontal boundary, temps \nshould remain below normal with highs in the 50s (terrain) to 60s \n(elsewhere) and lows in the upper 30s to 40s each day/night.",
